574 0 obj
<<
/Title (Example: If a driver can be registered only when a cab is assigned, the system may be unable to store a newly hired driver before a vehicle is assigned. Similarly, if driver details were stored only in BOOKING, a driver could not be recorded until the driver receives a booking.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 494 0 R
/Prev 573 0 R
/Next 544 0 R
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Title (A deletionanomalyoccurswhen removing one record unintentionally removes another fact that should)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 545 0 R
/Next 587 0 R
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Title (Example: If driver details were stored only in BOOKING, deleting the driver's last booking could also remove the only stored copy of that driver's name and phone number. Likewise, if a customer's profile were stored only alongside bookings, deleting the customer's last booking could erase the profile. Keeping CUSTOMER and DRIVER as separate tables helps prevent this. Deleting a booking should not automatically delete the associated customer, driver, or cab master record. Foreign-key deletion rules should be chosen carefully.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 545 0 R
/Prev 587 0 R
>>
endobj
